  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "The real value of \\(x\\) such that \\(64^{x-1}\\) divided by \\(4^{x-1}\\) equals \\(256^{2x}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac23\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac13\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac14\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac38\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "All three bases are powers of \\(2\\), so rewriting them with that common base makes their exponents comparable. Since \\(64=2^6\\), \\(4=2^2\\), and \\(256=2^8\\), the equation becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{2^{6(x-1)}}{2^{2(x-1)}}=2^{16x},\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2^{4x-4}=2^{16x}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is positive for every real \\(x\\), so these transformations are valid throughout the stated domain. Because \\(2^t\\) is strictly increasing, equal powers of \\(2\\) have equal exponents. Therefore\n\\[\n4x-4=16x,\n\\qquad -4=12x,\n\\qquad x=\\boxed{-\\frac13}.\n\\]\nEvery transformation is reversible, so this is the unique real solution, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
